{"type":"standard","title":"Šauška","displaytitle":"<span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Šauška</span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q391333","titles":{"canonical":"Šauška","normalized":"Šauška","display":"<span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Šauška</span>"},"pageid":9936323,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Shaushka_Yazilikaya.jpg/320px-Shaushka_Yazilikaya.jpg","width":320,"height":513},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Shaushka_Yazilikaya.jpg","width":690,"height":1107},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1247326076","tid":"768d7a2c-79ec-11ef-90e0-c04e46204447","timestamp":"2024-09-23T20:43:18Z","description":"Hurrian goddess of love and war","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka","revisions":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka","edit":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka?action=edit","talk":"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:%C5%A0au%C5%A1ka"}},"extract":"Šauška (Shaushka), also called Šauša or Šawuška, was the highest ranked goddess in the Hurrian pantheon. She was associated with love and war, as well as with incantations and by extension with healing. While she was usually referred to as a goddess and with feminine titles, such as allai, references to masculine Šauška are also known. The Hurrians associated her with Nineveh, but she was also worshiped in many other centers associated with this culture, from Anatolian cities in Kizzuwatna, through Alalakh and Ugarit in Syria, to Nuzi and Ulamme in northeastern Mesopotamia. She was also worshiped in southern Mesopotamia, where she was introduced alongside a number of other foreign deities in the Ur III period. In this area, she came to be associated with Ishtar. At a later point in time, growing Hurrian influence on Hittite culture resulted in the adoption of Šauška into the Hittite state pantheon.","extract_html":"<p><b>Šauška</b> (<b>Shaushka</b>), also called <b>Šauša</b> or <b>Šawuška</b>, was the highest ranked goddess in the Hurrian pantheon. She was associated with love and war, as well as with incantations and by extension with healing. While she was usually referred to as a goddess and with feminine titles, such as <i>allai</i>, references to masculine Šauška are also known. The Hurrians associated her with Nineveh, but she was also worshiped in many other centers associated with this culture, from Anatolian cities in Kizzuwatna, through Alalakh and Ugarit in Syria, to Nuzi and Ulamme in northeastern Mesopotamia. She was also worshiped in southern Mesopotamia, where she was introduced alongside a number of other foreign deities in the Ur III period. In this area, she came to be associated with Ishtar. At a later point in time, growing Hurrian influence on Hittite culture resulted in the adoption of Šauška into the Hittite state pantheon.</p>"}